    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-014",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Attribution: High Roller Ferris Wheel, by thiemen. Public Domain. Pixabay. Source.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-015",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The world’s tallest Ferris wheel is in Las Vegas. The height \\(h\\) in feet of one of the passenger seats on the Ferris wheel can be modeled by the function \\(f(t) = 275+ 260 \\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi t}{30}\\right)\\) where time \\(t\\) is measured…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-016",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What is the diameter of the Ferris wheel? Explain how you know.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "How long does it take the Ferris wheel to make a complete revolution? Explain how you know.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Give at least three different times when the passenger seat modeled by \\(f\\) is at its lowest point. Explain how you know.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Sketch a graph of the height of the seat on the Ferris wheel for at least two full revolutions.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here is a graph of a wave where the amplitude is not constant but rather decreases over time. Write an equation which could match this graph.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-021",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Here is a point \\(P\\) on a wheel.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-022",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Imagine the height \\(h\\) of \\(P\\) in feet relative to the center of the wheel after \\(t\\) seconds is given by the equation \\(h= \\sin(2\\pi t)\\). When \\(t = 1\\), that is after 1 second, the wheel will be back where it started. It will return…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-023",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "What if \\(h= \\sin(4\\pi t)\\) was the equation representing the height of \\(P\\) instead? What does this equation tell us about how long it takes the wheel to complete a full revolution? In this case, when \\(t = 1\\) the wheel has made 2…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-024",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Notice that the midline is the \\(t\\)-axis for each function and the amplitude is 1. The only difference is the period, how long it takes each function to complete one full revolution.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-025",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Now let's consider the height of a point on a wheel with a radius of 11 inches, the center of the wheel 11 inches off the ground, and completing two revolutions per second.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-026",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Notice that the midline and amplitude are 11. An equation defining this graph is \\(h = 11\\sin\\left(4\\pi t\\right)+11\\) where \\(h\\) is the height, in inches, of the point on the wheel after \\(t\\) seconds.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-027",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The maximum distance of the values of a periodic function above or below the midline.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U06-L16-ACTIVITY-028",
      "SourceSection": "16.3: Around the World’s Largest Ferris Wheel",
      "SourceExcerpt": "The value halfway between the maximum and minimum values of a period function. Also the horizontal line whose \\(y\\)-coordinate is that value.",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u06-l16.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
